course: dictionary
lesson: 11885
title: Breeches and old clothes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to the past. When you meet one you do not know, read the words around it for clues. «Tom pulled on his breeches, which stopped just below his knees.» The sentence says the breeches stop below the knees, so they must be a kind of trousers.
explain: Breeches are short trousers. Riders still wear them today for horse riding. «The rider wore tight breeches and tall black boots.» The word tight and the boots show that breeches for riding fit close to the leg.
explain: Old books also use breeches in a special phrase. «Stop being so proud, you are too big for your breeches!» The words too proud are the clue. Being too big for your breeches means thinking you are better than others.
fact: «Breeches» is a noun, and it is always plural: you wear a pair of breeches.
fact: «Breeches» means short trousers fastened just below the knee.
fact: «Breeches» can also mean trousers, but this use is informal and old-fashioned.
fact: Tudor, Stuart and Georgian men wore breeches with long stockings.
fact: «Too big for your breeches» means too proud of yourself.
exercise: «Tom pulled on his breeches, which stopped just below his knees.» What are breeches? | *Short trousers fastened just below the knee | A type of hat | Heavy boots for walking in the rain | Long socks worn in bed
exercise: «The rider wore tight breeches and tall black boots.» Why might a rider wear breeches? | To eat lunch | To keep dry in a rainstorm | To swim in the sea | *For horse riding
exercise: Lily says, «Stop being so proud, you are too big for your breeches!» What does this mean? | *You are too proud of yourself | You have grown taller since last year | You need a new pair of shoes | Your trousers are too large
test: In the history book, Tudor men wore breeches with long stockings. Which clothes were worn with breeches? | A raincoat | A wide woolly scarf | Sandals and a sun hat | *Long stockings
test: Sam was breeched at about six years old. What does breeched mean? | Given a haircut | Taught to read | *Put into breeches for the first time | Sent to bed early
exam: Which sentence uses breeches correctly? | The breeches were the loudest part of the storm | We ate breeches for our tea | The breeches sang a song in the garden | *The gentleman in the painting wore red breeches and a white shirt
